Overview Goal: the server checks real tokens; you can get one from the terminal. Check: the demo in Overview. Action File HoldsEdit src/main.ts Requires the Firebase key, builds the verifier and the S3 storage New scripts/dev-token.ts Signs in a test user with the Firebase REST API, prints the ID token Edit .env FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_BASE64, FIREBASE_WEB_API_KEY (from F00a) Steps 4.1 Main and token script File · Edit · src/main.ts @@ -1,4 +1,6 @@ import { buildApp } from './app.js'; +import { createFirebaseVerifier } from './auth.js'; import { loadConfig } from './config.js'; import { createPool } from './db.js'; +import { Storage, createS3Client } from './storage.js'; @@ -6,5 +8,14 @@ const config = loadConfig(); +if (config.firebaseServiceAccountBase64 === undefined) { + throw new Error('Missing environment variable FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_BASE64'); +} const pool = createPool(config); -const app = await buildApp({ config, pool }); +const tokens = createFirebaseVerifier(config.firebaseServiceAccountBase64); +const storage = new Storage( + createS3Client(config.awsRegion), + config.s3Bucket, + config.presignTtlSeconds, +); +const app = await buildApp({ config, pool, tokens, storage }); Code import { buildApp } from './app.js'; import { createFirebaseVerifier } from './auth.js'; import { loadConfig } from './config.js'; import { createPool } from './db.js'; import { Storage, createS3Client } from './storage.js'; // Starts the HTTP server. const config = loadConfig(); if (config.firebaseServiceAccountBase64 === undefined) { throw new Error('Missing environment variable FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_BASE64'); } const pool = createPool(config); const tokens = createFirebaseVerifier(config.firebaseServiceAccountBase64); const storage = new Storage( createS3Client(config.awsRegion), config.s3Bucket, config.presignTtlSeconds, ); const app = await buildApp({ config, pool, tokens, storage }); await app.listen({ host: '0.0.0.0', port: config.port }); // docker stop sends SIGTERM: finish open requests, then close the database pool. async function shutdown(signal: string): Promise<void> { app.log.info({ signal }, 'shutting down'); await app.close(); await pool.end(); } process.once('SIGTERM', () => void shutdown('SIGTERM')); process.once('SIGINT', () => void shutdown('SIGINT')); File · New · scripts/dev-token.ts Explain · The same sign-in the app does, from the terminal, for curl. The Web API key is not a secret (it ships inside every app), but keep it in .env anyway. Code // Prints a Firebase ID token for a test user, for curl: npm run token -- you@example.com password // Uses the dev Firebase project's Web API key (Firebase console → Project settings → General). const apiKey = process.env.FIREBASE_WEB_API_KEY; const email = process.argv[2]; const password = process.argv[3]; if (apiKey === undefined || email === undefined || password === undefined) { process.stderr.write('Usage: FIREBASE_WEB_API_KEY=… npm run token -- <email> <password>
'); process.exit(1); } const response = await fetch( `https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com/v1/accounts:signInWithPassword?key=${apiKey}`, { method: 'POST', headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify({ email, password, returnSecureToken: true }), }, ); const body = (await response.json()) as { idToken?: string; error?: { message: string } }; if (body.idToken === undefined) { process.stderr.write(`Sign-in failed: ${body.error?.message ?? response.status}
`); process.exit(1); } process.stdout.write(`${body.idToken}
`); npm run dev curl -i -X DELETE localhost:3000/account TOKEN=$(npm run -s token -- test1@example.com '<password>') curl -s -X DELETE localhost:3000/account -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" Expected: {"deletedObjects":0,"abortedUploads":0,"deletedRows":0}, and test1@example.com is gone from Firebase → Authentication → Users. Add it again for later steps. Try Run the last curl again with the same token → 401: the user was deleted, so the token is revoked.
